<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<chapter xml:id="graphics">
  <title>Drawing graphs</title>
  <blockquote>
    <p><em>Above all else show the data.</em> --Edward Tufte<fn>The origin of this quote is Tufte's lovely book <em>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information</em>.</fn></p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Visualising data is one of the most important tasks facing the data analyst. It's important for two distinct but closely related reasons. Firstly, there's the matter of drawing "presentation graphics": displaying your data in a clean, visually appealing fashion makes it easier for your reader to understand what you're trying to tell them. Equally important, perhaps even more important, is the fact that drawing graphs helps <em>you</em> to understand the data. To that end, it's important to draw "exploratory graphics" that help you learn about the data as you go about analysing it. These points might seem pretty obvious, but I cannot count the number of times I've seen people forget them.
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="fig-snowmap1">
    <caption>A stylised redrawing of John Snow's original cholera map. Each small dot represents the location of a cholera case, and each large circle shows the location of a well. As the plot makes clear, the cholera outbreak is centred very closely on the Broad St pump.  This image uses the data from the <c>HistData</c> package, and was drawn using minor alterations to the commands provided in the help files. Note that Snow's original hand drawn map used different symbols and labels, but you get the idea.</caption>
    <image source="generated/snowmap1.png"/>
  </figure>
  <p>
    To give a sense of the importance of this chapter, I want to start with a classic illustration of just how powerful a good graph can be. To that end, <xref ref="fig-snowmap1" /> shows a redrawing of one of the most famous data visualisations of all time: John Snow's 1854 map of cholera deaths. The map is elegant in its simplicity. In the background we have a street map, which helps orient the viewer. Over the top, we see a large number of small dots, each one representing the location of a cholera case. The larger symbols show the location of water pumps, labelled by name. Even the most casual inspection of the graph makes it very clear that the source of the outbreak is almost certainly the Broad Street pump. Upon viewing this graph, Dr Snow arranged to have the handle removed from the pump, ending the outbreak that had killed over 500 people. Such is the power of a good data visualisation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The goals in this chapter are twofold: firstly, to discuss several fairly standard graphs that we use a lot when analysing and presenting data, and secondly, to show you how to create these graphs in R-Instat. The graphs themselves tend to be pretty straightforward, so in that respect this chapter is pretty simple. Where people usually struggle is learning how to produce graphs, and especially, learning how to produce good graphs.<fn>I should add that this isn't unique to R-Instat. I've seen the same problems show up regardless of what system people use. I suspect that the hardest thing to do is to force yourself to take the time to think deeply about what your graphs are doing. I say that in full knowledge that only about half of my graphs turn out as well as they ought to. Understanding what makes a good graph is easy: actually designing a good graph is <em>hard</em>.</fn> Fortunately, learning how to draw graphs in R-Instat is reasonably simple, as long as you're not too picky about what your graph looks like on the first try. Most of the time you can produce a clean, high-quality graphic from the menus without touching any code. When you do want more control, the same dialogs offer a <term>Plot Options</term> sub-dialog for titles, themes, colours, and faceting. With that in mind, the structure of this chapter is as follows: I'll start with a quick overview of how graphing works in R-Instat, then walk through histograms, stem-and-leaf plots, boxplots, scatterplots, and bar graphs (including pie charts), and finish with saving and customising your figures.
  </p>
  <section xml:id="rgraphics">
    <title>An overview of graphics in R-Instat</title>
    <p>
      In R-Instat, you do not type plotting commands. Instead, you open a graphing dialog from the menus, choose variables in the receivers on the right, adjust any options you need, and click <term>OK</term>. The graph appears in the <term>Output Window</term>, alongside tables and other results from the same session.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of the graphs in this chapter live under <term>Describe > Graphs</term>. That submenu gives you dedicated dialogs for the common plot types we need: <term>Histogram...</term>, <term>Boxplot...</term>, <term>Scatter Plot...</term>, and <term>Bar Chart...</term>. There is also a more general <term>Describe > One Variable > Graph...</term> dialog, which is useful when you want several one-variable plot types (including histograms and facets) from a single entry point; we used that dialog in <xref ref="descriptives"/> for faceted histograms of the <c>parenthood</c> data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every dedicated graph dialog follows the same basic layout. On the left is a data-frame selector and list of variables. On the right are one or more <term>receivers</term> where you place the variables that define the plot. At the bottom you will usually find a <term>Store Graph</term> checkbox: when it is checked, R-Instat saves the graph under a name you choose so you can refer to it later (for example when exporting or combining graphs). Next to the main options you will often see a <term>Plot Options</term> button (sometimes with a small dropdown arrow for additional layer-specific options). That button opens the same style of sub-dialog across many plots, with tabs for titles, axes, themes, colours, facets, and more.
    </p>
    <remark xml:id="rem-recent-dialogs-graphics">
      <title>Reopening a recent dialog</title>
      <p>
        If you have just drawn a histogram and want to tweak it, you do not need to navigate through <term>Describe > Graphs > Histogram...</term> again. Use the toolbar button <term>View/Edit Last Dialogue</term>: click the main part of the button to reopen the most recent dialog, or the dropdown arrow to pick from the last ten dialogs.
      </p>
    </remark>
    <p>
      Compared with typing R commands directly, this workflow trades some fine-grained control for consistency. You will not need to learn about R's separate graphics systems, devices, or the low-level <c>par()</c> function. Those ideas matter for advanced R programmers, but they are not part of the R-Instat user experience described in this book.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="introplotting">
    <title>Customising plots with Plot Options</title>
    <p>
      Before we look at specific graph types, it is worth knowing where the common customisation options live. In the original R version of this book, the opening sections walked through dozens of arguments to the <c>plot()</c> function <mdash /> titles, axis labels, colours, line types, and so on. In R-Instat, the same kinds of choices are spread across the main dialog and the <term>Plot Options</term> sub-dialog.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open any graph dialog that has a <term>Plot Options</term> button <mdash /> for example <term>Describe > Graphs > Scatter Plot...</term> <mdash /> and click it. The sub-dialog title is <term>Plot Options</term>. The tabs you will use most often are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><term>Titles</term> <mdash /> set the graph title, subtitle, caption, and axis labels.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Themes</term> <mdash /> choose an overall visual style for the plot.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Facets</term> <mdash /> split the plot into panels by one or two factor variables (<term>1st Factor</term> and <term>2nd Factor (Optional)</term>), with layout by <term>Row</term> or <term>Column</term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>X-Axis</term> and <term>Y-Axis</term> <mdash /> control axis text, limits, and related display options.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Colour</term> <mdash /> adjust colour palettes and fill scales.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Many dialogs also expose a few high-use options on the main screen so you do not have to open the sub-dialog for every change. For example, the <term>Histogram Plot</term> dialog has <term>Legend:</term> with a position dropdown, and <term>Facet By:</term> with <term>1st Factor</term> and <term>2nd Factor (Optional)</term> receivers, matching the facet controls inside <term>Plot Options</term>. The <term>Boxplot</term> dialog has the same <term>Legend:</term> and <term>Facet By:</term> pattern, plus plot-type buttons for <term>Boxplot</term>, <term>Jitter Plot</term>, and <term>Violin Plot</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not feel you need to master every tab before drawing your first graph. The defaults are sensible for exploratory work. Come back to <term>Plot Options</term> when you need a publication-quality title, a different theme, or a faceted layout <mdash /> the specialised sections later in this chapter will point you to the relevant controls for each plot type.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="hist">
    <title>Histograms</title>
    <p>
      We begin with the humble <term>histogram</term>. Histograms are one of the simplest and most useful ways of visualising data. They make most sense when you have an interval or ratio scale variable (for example, the <c>afl.margins</c> data from <xref ref="descriptives"/>) and you want to get an overall impression of the distribution. All you do is divide up the possible values into <term>bins</term>, and then count the number of observations that fall within each bin. This count is referred to as the frequency of the bin, and is displayed as a bar: in the AFL winning margins data, there are 33 games in which the winning margin was less than 10 points, and it is this fact that is represented by the height of the leftmost bar in <xref ref="fig-hist1a" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      To draw a histogram in R-Instat, make sure the <c>afl.margins</c> data frame is loaded (see <xref ref="descriptives"/>), then use <term>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; Histogram...</term>. The dialog title is <term>Histogram Plot</term>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>At the top of the dialog, ensure the <term>Histogram</term> plot type is selected (the other options are <term>Density/Ridges</term> and <term>Frequency Polygon</term>).</p></li>
      <li><p>Move <c>afl.margins</c> into the variable receiver on the right.</p></li>
      <li><p>Click <term>OK</term>. The histogram appears in the Output Window, similar to <xref ref="fig-hist1a" />.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <figure xml:id="fig-hist1a">
      <caption>A histogram of the AFL 2010 winning margins (<c>afl.margins</c>). This illustration is from the original <em>Learning Statistics with R</em> book; your R-Instat output will follow the same general shape with ggplot2-style defaults.</caption>
      <image source="generated/hist1a.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Although the figure may need titles and axis labels before you include it in a report, it does a pretty good job of describing the data. The big strength of a histogram is that (properly used) it shows the entire spread of the data. The downside is that histograms are not very compact: it is hard to cram 20–30 histograms into one image without overwhelming the viewer. And of course, if your data are nominal scale (for example, <c>afl.finalists</c>) then histograms are useless.
    </p>
    <p>
      The main subtlety when drawing histograms is choosing sensible bin widths. <xref ref="fig-hist1a" /> uses bins at 0, 10, 20, … 120, which is reasonable for these data. By contrast, <xref ref="fig-hist1b" /> uses far too few bins and <xref ref="fig-hist1c" /> uses far too many. In R-Instat you can control this with the <term>Binwidth</term> checkbox on the main dialog: check it and enter a width (for example, 10 for decade-sized bins, or a smaller value if you want finer detail).
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-hist1b">
      <caption>A histogram with too few bins (conceptual illustration from the original book).</caption>
      <image source="generated/hist1b.png"/>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-hist1c">
      <caption>A histogram with too many bins (conceptual illustration from the original book).</caption>
      <image source="generated/hist1c.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      In short, the bin width has a big effect on what the histogram looks like, so it is worth experimenting. R-Instat picks a default bin width automatically when <term>Binwidth</term> is unchecked; that is usually fine for a first look.
    </p>
    <subsection>
      <title>Visual style of your histogram</title>
      <p>
        The default histogram is fine for exploration, but you will often want a cleaner presentation graphic. Use the same <term>Histogram Plot</term> dialog, then:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Click <term>Plot Options</term> and open the <term>Titles</term> tab to set the graph title and axis labels (for example, title <q>2010 AFL margins</q>, x-axis <q>Margin</q>).</p></li>
        <li><p>On the <term>Themes</term> or <term>Colour</term> tab, adjust fill colour and borders if you want a styled version like <xref ref="fig-hist1d" />.</p></li>
        <li><p>On the main dialog, use <term>Legend:</term> and <term>Facet By:</term> if you are colouring or splitting the plot by a factor.</p></li>
        <li><p>Check <term>Store Graph</term> at the bottom if you want to save the result under a name for later export.</p></li>
      </ul>
      <figure xml:id="fig-hist1d">
        <caption>A styled histogram (conceptual illustration from the original book). In R-Instat, achieve similar results through <term>Plot Options</term> rather than typing plotting commands.</caption>
        <image source="generated/hist1d.png"/>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The dialog also offers <term>Display as Dotplot</term> (still under the <term>Histogram</term> plot type) and, under <term>Density/Ridges</term>, a density or ridge plot if you want a smoothed view of the same variable. Those are useful alternatives when a standard histogram feels too chunky.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="stem">
    <title>Stem and leaf plots</title>
    <p>
      Histograms are widely used, but sometimes a simpler display is enough. A <term>stem and leaf plot</term> is roughly a text-based histogram: each observation appears in the display, so you can read off individual values. Stem and leaf plots are less common today than they were decades ago, and they do not work well for large data sets. The AFL margins data (176 observations) is at the upper end of what is practical. You may still see them in older publications.
    </p>
    <p>
      In R-Instat, stem-and-leaf output is produced from the <term>Frequencies</term> dialog, not from the main <term>Graphs</term> submenu. Open <term>Describe &gt; One Variable &gt; Frequencies...</term>, select the <c>afl.margins</c> data frame, and click the <term>Stem and Leaf</term> button at the top of the dialog (alongside <term>Table</term> and <term>Graph</term>).
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Move one or more numeric variables (for example, <c>afl.margins</c>) into the <term>Selected Variable:</term> receiver.</p></li>
      <li><p>Optionally check <term>Scale</term> or <term>Width</term> to adjust how the stems are grouped (these correspond to the scale and width arguments in R's classic <c>stem()</c> function).</p></li>
      <li><p>Click <term>OK</term>. The stem-and-leaf display appears in the Output Window as text, not as a ggplot figure.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The values to the left of the <c>|</c> are <term>stems</term> and the digits to the right are <term>leaves</term>. If you read down the leaves on each line, you recover the original observations. For example, a line like <c>10 | 148</c> means there were games with winning margins of 101, 104, and 108; the line <c>11 | 6</c> corresponds to the maximum margin of 116 in this data set.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the output is text in the Output Window, there is no <term>Store Graph</term> checkbox for this mode. If you need to keep the display, copy it from the output or take a screenshot.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="boxplots">
    <title>Boxplots</title>
    <p>
      Another alternative to histograms is a <term>boxplot</term>, sometimes called a <q>box and whiskers</q> plot. Boxplots summarise the median, the interquartile range, and the spread of the data in a compact form. They are especially popular during exploratory analysis. We again use <c>afl.margins</c> from <xref ref="descriptives"/>; the median is about 30.5 and the IQR runs from roughly 13 to 50.5 (values you can confirm in the <term>Summarise</term> dialog from Chapter 5).
    </p>
    <p>
      Open <term>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; Boxplot...</term>. At the top, choose the plot type: <term>Boxplot</term> (default), <term>Jitter Plot</term>, or <term>Violin Plot</term>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Ensure <term>Boxplot</term> is selected.</p></li>
      <li><p>Move <c>afl.margins</c> into the <term>Variable:</term> receiver.</p></li>
      <li><p>Click <term>OK</term> to produce a basic boxplot like <xref ref="fig-boxplot1a" />.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <figure xml:id="fig-boxplot1a">
      <caption>A basic boxplot of <c>afl.margins</c> (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/boxplot1a.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      When you read a boxplot, the thick line in the box is the median; the box spans the 25th to 75th percentiles; the whiskers extend to the most extreme points within a default distance of 1.5 × IQR from the box edges. Points beyond that are drawn separately as outliers. See the annotated diagram in <xref ref="fig-boxplot1b" />.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-boxplot1b">
      <caption>An annotated boxplot (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/boxplot1_annotated.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      For <c>afl.margins</c>, one game (margin 116) falls outside the usual whisker range and appears as an outlier, as in <xref ref="fig-boxplot2a" />. You can change the rule with <term>Outlier Coefficient :</term> on the main dialog (the default matches the usual 1.5 × IQR rule). Check <term>Label Outliers</term> and move a label variable into the <term>Variable:</term> receiver below it if you want to identify which row each outlier came from.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-boxplot2a">
      <caption>Default boxplot with one outlier separated (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/boxplot2a.png"/>
    </figure>
    <subsection>
      <title>Visual style of your boxplot</title>
      <p>
        Useful options on the main <term>Boxplot</term> dialog include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><term>Variable Width</term> — when you have several boxes, scale each box's width by the number of observations.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Horizontal Plot</term> — draw the boxplot sideways (helpful when category labels are long).</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Add Points</term> — overlay individual data points on the boxplot.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Tufte Boxplots</term> — an alternative minimal boxplot style.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Legend:</term> and <term>Facet By:</term> — same pattern as the histogram dialog; see <xref ref="introplotting" />.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Plot Options</term> — titles, themes, colours, and axis limits.</p></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A cleaned-up version with subdued whiskers and emphasised median might look like <xref ref="fig-boxplot2b" />; in R-Instat you would get there through <term>Plot Options</term> and the boxplot-specific options in the <term>Plot Options</term> dropdown (for example <term>Box Options</term>), rather than by memorising dozens of low-level graphics parameters.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-boxplot2b">
        <caption>A customised boxplot (conceptual illustration from the original book).</caption>
        <image source="generated/boxplot2b.png"/>
      </figure>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="boxplotoutliers">
      <title>Using box plots to detect outliers</title>
      <p>
        Because boxplots automatically flag distant observations, people often use them informally to spot <term>outliers</term>. Suppose the boxplot looked like <xref ref="fig-boxplotoutlier" /> — clearly something is wrong if a winning margin exceeds 300 points.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-boxplotoutlier">
        <caption>A boxplot showing suspicious outliers (conceptual illustration).</caption>
        <image source="generated/boxplotoutlier.png"/>
      </figure>
      <p>
        In R-Instat, enable <term>Label Outliers</term> on the <term>Boxplot</term> dialog to see which row each outlier corresponds to, then inspect that row in the Data View. You can also use <term>Prepare &gt; Filter...</term> to restrict the data frame to extreme values and track down typos. Real data sets are often riddled with data-entry errors; cleaning them can take a large part of an analysis. This phase is sometimes called <term>data cleaning</term>.
      </p>
      <p>
        What about the real outlier at 116 in <c>afl.margins</c>? It is unusual but explainable (a late-season game where Fremantle rested players and Hawthorn won heavily). Whether to exclude such a point depends on your research question — statistical software can flag candidates, but the decision is yours. I return to this theme in <xref ref="regressiondiagnostics" />.
      </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="multipleboxplots">
      <title>Drawing multiple boxplots</title>
      <p>
        Suppose you want separate boxplots of winning margin for each AFL season from 1987 to 2010. Load the <c>afl2</c> data frame from <c>lsrdata</c> if you have not already (<term>File &gt; Import From Library...</term>, package <c>lsrdata</c>, data set <c>afl2</c>). It contains thousands of rows with variables <c>margin</c> and <c>year</c>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open <term>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; Boxplot...</term>, select the <c>afl2</c> data frame, and:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Move <c>margin</c> into the <term>Variable:</term> receiver.</p></li>
        <li><p>Move <c>year</c> into the <term>Factor (Usually):</term> receiver. R-Instat draws one boxplot per year.</p></li>
        <li><p>Optionally check <term>Variable Width</term> so wider boxes reflect larger sample sizes within each year.</p></li>
        <li><p>Use <term>Plot Options</term> to add axis labels (for example, x-axis <q>AFL season</q>, y-axis <q>Winning margin</q>).</p></li>
        <li><p>Click <term>OK</term>. The result is similar to <xref ref="fig-multipleboxplots" />.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <figure xml:id="fig-multipleboxplots">
        <caption>Boxplots of AFL winning margins by year (conceptual illustration).</caption>
        <image source="generated/multipleboxplots.png"/>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Even a default faceted or grouped boxplot layout makes it easier to compare years than trying to fit 24 histograms side by side. With <term>Plot Options</term> you can refine colours and themes; <xref ref="fig-multipleboxplots2" /> shows the kind of polished version you might aim for.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-multipleboxplots2">
        <caption>A refined multi-year boxplot layout (conceptual illustration from the original book).</caption>
        <image source="generated/multipleboxplots2.png"/>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The <term>Violin Plot</term> and <term>Jitter Plot</term> buttons at the top of the same dialog combine well with boxplots: for example, select <term>Violin Plot</term> and check <term>Add Boxplot</term> and <term>Add Points</term> to show the distribution shape, the quartiles, and the raw data together (similar to what jamovi users get by ticking several plot checkboxes at once).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="scatterplots">
    <title>Scatterplots</title>
    <p>
      <term>Scatterplots</term> display the relationship between two numeric variables: each observation is a point, with one variable on the x-axis and one on the y-axis. We saw the idea in <xref ref="correl"/> with the <c>parenthood</c> data. If you have not loaded it, import <c>parenthood</c> from <c>lsrdata</c> via <term>File &gt; Import From Library...</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open <term>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; Scatter Plot...</term>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Select the <c>parenthood</c> data frame.</p></li>
      <li><p>Move <c>dan.grump</c> into the main variable receiver (y-axis).</p></li>
      <li><p>Move <c>dan.sleep</c> into the <term>X Variable:</term> receiver.</p></li>
      <li><p>Under <term>Geom</term>, leave <term>Point</term> selected (the default).</p></li>
      <li><p>Click <term>OK</term> for a basic plot like <xref ref="fig-scattera" />.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <figure xml:id="fig-scattera">
      <caption>A default scatterplot of grumpiness versus sleep (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/scattera.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      To improve the presentation, reopen the dialog (or use <term>View/Edit Last Dialogue</term>) and:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Open <term>Plot Options</term> and set axis labels (for example, <q>My sleep (hours)</q> and <q>My grumpiness (0–100)</q>) on the <term>Titles</term> tab, and axis limits on the <term>X-Axis</term> and <term>Y-Axis</term> tabs if you want fixed ranges such as 0–12 and 0–100.</p></li>
      <li><p>Under the <term>Add</term> group on the main dialog, check <term>Line of Best Fit</term> to overlay a linear trend (similar to adding a regression line in R). Optionally check <term>With Standard Error</term> for a confidence band.</p></li>
      <li><p>Check <term>Rug Plot</term> under <term>Add</term> if you want tick marks along the axes showing where observations fall.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <figure xml:id="fig-scatterb">
      <caption>A scatterplot with labels and a line of best fit (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/scatterb.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Move a factor into <term>By Variable (Optional):</term> to colour points by group, or use <term>Facet By:</term> for separate panels. Check <term>Store Graph</term> when you want to keep the figure for export.
    </p>
    <subsection>
      <title>More elaborate options</title>
      <p>
        When you want to look at several numeric variables at once, a <term>scatterplot matrix</term> shows every pair of variables in one grid (the graphical counterpart of a correlation matrix from <xref ref="correl"/>). R-Instat does not offer a single menu item that exactly duplicates R's <c>pairs()</c> function. For a full matrix of scatterplots, explore <term>Describe &gt; Multivariate &gt; Correlations...</term> and related multivariate graphs, or build several scatterplots with <term>Facet By:</term>. For the two-variable case that covers most introductory work, the <term>Scatter Plot</term> dialog is the right tool.
      </p>
      <p>
        Under <term>Geom</term>, <term>Jitter</term> and <term>Count</term> are useful when points overlap heavily. The <term>Plot Options</term> dropdown also includes layer-specific entries (for example <term>Point Options</term>, <term>Smooth Options</term> when a line of best fit is enabled).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="bargraph">
    <title>Bar graphs and pie charts</title>
    <p>
      Another common graph is the <term>bar graph</term>, used to display counts or summaries for categorical variables. We use <c>afl.finalists</c> from <xref ref="descriptives"/>, which lists every team that appeared in an AFL final from 1987–2010. The goal is a bar chart showing how many finals each team played in.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open <term>Describe &gt; Graphs &gt; Bar Chart...</term> (dialog title: <term>Bar Chart</term>).
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Ensure the <term>Frequency</term> mode is selected at the top (as opposed to <term>Value</term>, which sums a numeric variable by category).</p></li>
      <li><p>Move <c>afl.finalists</c> into the <term>X Variable:</term> receiver.</p></li>
      <li><p>Click <term>OK</term>. R-Instat counts how often each team appears and draws a bar chart like <xref ref="fig-bar1a" /> (with team names on the axis once labels fit).</p></li>
    </ol>
    <figure xml:id="fig-bar1a">
      <caption>A simple bar chart of finals counts (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/bar1a.png"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Long team names can make x-axis labels overlap. On the main dialog, try <term>Reorder</term> to sort bars by frequency, and open <term>Plot Options</term> to rotate axis text or increase bottom margin space via theme settings. <term>Flip Coordinates</term> (on the main dialog) swaps axes so labels read horizontally along the y-axis — often the easiest fix when there are many categories, as in <xref ref="fig-bar1c" /> and <xref ref="fig-bar1d" />.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-bar1b">
      <caption>Bar chart with horizontal category labels (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/bar1b.png"/>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-bar1c">
      <caption>Rotated labels when space is tight (conceptual illustration).</caption>
      <image source="generated/bar1c.png"/>
    </figure>
    <subsection xml:id="par">
      <title>Customising bar charts and pie charts</title>
      <p>
        In the original R version of this book, adjusting margins for crowded bar labels meant calling the low-level <c>par()</c> function before plotting. In R-Instat you handle the same problems through <term>Plot Options</term> (axis text angle, titles, themes) and dialog options such as <term>Flip Coordinates</term> and <term>Add Labels</term>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a pie chart of the same counts, reopen <term>Bar Chart...</term>, move <c>afl.finalists</c> into <term>X Variable:</term>, and select the <term>Pie</term> radio button (there is also a <term>Donut</term> option). Pie charts are controversial among statisticians — bar charts are usually easier to read — but the dialog supports both.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-bar1d">
        <caption>A bar chart with expanded margins and styling (conceptual illustration from the original book).</caption>
        <image source="generated/bar1d.png"/>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Check <term>Store Graph</term> when you are happy with the chart. Use <term>Plot Options</term> for the graph title (for example, <q>Finals played, 1987–2010</q>) and y-axis label (<q>Number of finals</q>).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="saveimage">
    <title>Saving graphs</title>
    <p>
      Drawing a graph in the Output Window is only half the job — you usually want to save it for a report or assignment. R-Instat offers two main routes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Storing the graph when you create it.</em> At the bottom of most graph dialogs (Histogram, Boxplot, Scatter Plot, Bar Chart, and others), check <term>Store Graph</term>. Choose a name in the text box or dropdown. The graph is saved as an R object linked to the data frame so you can refer to it later.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Exporting to an image file.</em> Use <term>File &gt; Export &gt; Export Graph As Image...</term>. In the <term>Export Graph As Image</term> dialog, select the stored graph in the <term>Selected Graph:</term> receiver, choose a file path and format, and click <term>OK</term>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That workflow replaces the RStudio <q>Export</q> button and the low-level R device commands from the original book. For most users, storing the graph at creation time and exporting once is all you need.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>
      Perhaps I'm a simple minded person, but I love pictures. Every time I write a new scientific paper, one of the first things I do is sit down and think about what the pictures will be. In my head, an article is really just a sequence of pictures, linked together by a story. All the rest of it is just window dressing. What I'm really trying to say here is that the human visual system is a very powerful data analysis tool. Give it the right kind of information and it will supply a human reader with a massive amount of knowledge very quickly. Not for nothing do we have the saying "a picture is worth a thousand words". With that in mind, I think that this is one of the most important chapters in the book. The topics covered were:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p><em>Graphics in R-Instat</em>. In <xref ref="rgraphics" /> we described how graphs appear in the Output Window and how the <term>Describe &gt; Graphs</term> menus fit together; in <xref ref="introplotting" /> we introduced the shared <term>Plot Options</term> sub-dialog.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><em>Common plots</em>. Histograms (<xref ref="hist" />), stem and leaf plots (<xref ref="stem" />), boxplots (<xref ref="boxplots" />), scatterplots (<xref ref="scatterplots" />), and bar graphs or pie charts (<xref ref="bargraph" />).</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><em>Saving graphs</em>. Using <term>Store Graph</term> when creating a figure and <term>File &gt; Export &gt; Export Graph As Image...</term> to write image files (<xref ref="saveimage" />).</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      R-Instat builds its graphs with ggplot2 under the hood, so the figures you get are part of the same modern graphics ecosystem used in much of the R world. You do not need to learn that system to use this book — the menus and <term>Plot Options</term> dialog are enough for the graphs we need here. If you later move on to writing R code directly, packages such as <c>ggplot2</c> (see <url href="https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/" visual="ggplot2.tidyverse.org"/>) extend what you have already seen in the dialogs.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
